## Go Exploration

When exploring Go code, use semantic tools to discover declarations and obtain precise ranges before reading implementation:

- Use `outline_package` first when a package is unfamiliar or you need to learn which declarations it contains.
- Use `find_symbol` when you know a type, function, method, constant, or variable name.
- Use the returned `file`, `start_line`, and `end_line` with `read_file` to read only the declarations relevant to the task.
- Use text search for references, call sites, struct or interface fields, literals, local declarations, or other queries the semantic tools do not support.

Prefer targeted range reads over whole-file reads. Broaden the range only when imports, surrounding declarations, or other context are necessary. If semantic lookup is unavailable or fails, fall back to text search.

